[Music]  I think open worlds are a very appealing  idea for gamers you're putting these  massive spaces with dozens of places to  visit seemingly endless quest to take on  and the freedom to explore how you want  developers seem to think so as well  because we've been getting an  oversaturation of open world games at  the plate and back during the 360 ps3  era when shooters were the most popular  genre trend started happening where  beloved game series like syndicate were  turned into first-person shooters this  upset a lot of people because what made  syndicate syndicate was gone today we  are seeing something similar with series  like Mirror's Edge and Metal Gear adding  open world while these are completely  changing genres the way syndicate did  metal gear is still about self and  mirrors edge is still about first person  parkour running adding an open world to  a series that never used one will alter  the design of the game sometimes this  works out great with series like far cry  we're adding an open world is the  logical next step to evolve its design  but other times the open world is  unnecessary or worse it removes elements  that make the series interesting and  replaces it with open world design we've  seen dozens of times before a game I  think completely justifies its  transition to open world is the witcher  3 wild hunt the core of the game is  still the same as the previous game you  have your main quest side quests and  master contract only now the space you  are given is significantly larger and  this larger space is used to expand  quest that couldn't be done previously  monster contracts becomes reeling chases  and side missions like a tower full of  mice get their own Island this massive  space also helps make the northern  realms feel like a real place by telling  the story of a country ravaged by war as  you see the effects on both the land and  its people comparatively Mirror's Edge  catalyst open-world the city of glass  feels unrelated to the rest of the game  the main story is a series of levels  that features straightforward pads with  increasing challenge to tester parkour  skills and they all take place in  locations outside of the open world they  don't take advantage of the open space  the city of glass gives and the city is  mostly they're to travel from mission to  Mission traversing the city lacks the  increasing parkour challenge of the  story missions and over the course of  the game you will travel the same paths  over and over making it quite boring the  city is populated with some running  challenges but personally I didn't find  them very interesting rather than  providing an exciting place for me to  explore the city of glass felt more like  an extra step I now needed to take to  get to the parts of the game I actually  wanted to see the good parts of the  original mirrors edge is still here  there's just now an extra barrier to get  some so while the core of mirrors edges  design doesn't really evolve with this  open-world conclusion metal gear solid 5  the Phantom Pain's does it's free form  open-world setting takes metal gears  focus on Claire experimentation and  places you in a true sandbox environment  to play around with how do you approach  a base do you cite from a distance or go  loud up close do you use D horse d dog d  walker quiet or maybe you just  the phantom pain uses this open-world to  allow players as many options as  possible and I think this is all  wonderful however the game suffers some  of the pitfalls at other open world  games have there are over 150 side ops  which is a real overload of missions and  it doesn't help that they start to  repeat in objectives very quickly if you  try and play the game to full completion  it can take you around 90 hours and is  easy to start getting sick of it this is  a far cry from the much tighter pacing  of previous Metal Gear games which would  take around 20 hours for a completionist  run another thing the phantom pain loses  is metal gears traditionally told  bombastic cinematic narrative cutscenes  are kept to a minimal and most of the  story is told through collectible tapes  your will buggiest up the invention all  right but I still don't get it now audio  logs aren't something that only open  world games doom but a lot have adopted  them because it's a great way to double  up on collectibles and narrative it also  allows players to experience the  narrative at their own pace which is  very much in line with open-world  freeform design now I found that they're  just personally wasn't enough narrative  they are compared to the previous games  to feel satisfying on this front and it  is the reason a lot of people play metal  gear but in other ways I'm okay with  this because the phantom pain is a game  that really gives the impression that it  chose to put all of its efforts into the  open world gameplay and that part of the  game really is quite good and then we  have Final Fantasy 15 and you could very  well make the argument that the series  has always been open world ish but in 15  s case it's specifically influenced by a  more Western approach to open world  design in a very unfun all fantasy move  it throws you into its open world within  the first 10 minutes of the game and  then gives you a large amount of freedom  and how you want to explore and I think  it all works quite well cruising the  world in your car is fun and the various  activities you are given like camping  fishing chocobo racing and frog  collecting I'll support the tone and  feeling of a road trip I think the team  makes a great argument for open-world  design in a final fantasy game where  fall short is how the narrative feels  disconnected from the open world itself  the major plots and events which include  an entire kingdom being overthrown don't  actually impact the inhabitants of the  world who still go about their day like  nothing happened the disconnect is  worsened when major narratives  sections take place outside of the open  world and linear environments you can't  return to and once the plot really  starts going at the end the open world  has taken away completely this  highlights a major weak point of open  world game design it's very difficult to  have a traditional large-scale story in  an open world because to make it really  pay off you have to have the world react  and respond to the story and that's a  lot of work so some games will choose to  focus on a series of smaller scale  stories that add up instead which is how  the witcher 3 does it or they decide to  tell their story through other means  entirely like Metal Gear Solid 5 s  collectibles and I good yes I became  obsessed with them I don't deny that  open roles can be downright magical when  done right but because the scale is so  much larger than typical games  developers really need to put in that  much more extra effort to get them right  but even then open world design is not  some seasoning you can just sprinkle on  to any game and make it work you have to  make concessions and I worry about what  these games are losing in these  transitions so instead of putting an  open world into their game because it's  the popular thing that people like I  think developers should ask what are the  benefits of going open world and what  are the drawbacks will this game be fun  to play an open role can we change it to  be fun and keep its identity because  sometimes these changes can take away  from what makes of that series special  in the first place and suddenly it just  looks like everything else

